<div class="faq-section mt-8 p-6 bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="mb-4 pb-4 border-b border-gray-200 last:border-b-0 last:mb-0 last:pb-0">
<h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">What is total daily energy expenditure?</h3>
<p>Total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) is the total number of calories your body burns in 24 hours. It comprises BMR (60-75% of TDEE), TEF (10%), NEAT (15-30%), and EAT (5-10%). Knowing your TDEE is the first step to setting an accurate calorie deficit for fat loss.</p>
</div>
<div class="mb-4 pb-4 border-b border-gray-200 last:border-b-0 last:mb-0 last:pb-0">
<h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">How does metabolism change during fat loss?</h3>
<p>As you lose weight, your metabolism naturally slows because a smaller body requires fewer calories. This is called metabolic adaptation. Research shows that after significant weight loss, resting metabolic rate can drop 10-20% more than expected based on weight loss alone, which is why adjusting your intake periodically is essential.</p>
</div>
<div class="mb-4 pb-4 border-b border-gray-200 last:border-b-0 last:mb-0 last:pb-0">
<h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">Can I boost my metabolism?</h3>
<p>While you cannot dramatically boost your metabolism, you can optimize it. Building muscle through resistance training increases BMR over time. Eating sufficient protein (1.6-2.2 g/kg) maximizes TEF. Maintaining NEAT through daily step goals and staying active prevents the natural decline in non-exercise activity during dieting.</p>
</div>
</div>

